\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Fipa is a Bantu language of the Ufipa plateau, Rukwa Region, Tanzania, with about 200,000 speakers. Very little was found; no school materials or dictionary appeared in search results.
\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None were found.
\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Overview: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fipa_language}
\item Colloquium abstract on Fipa dialectal and syntactic variation (Humboldt-Universit\"at): \url{https://iaaw.hu-berlin.de/de/afrika/linguistik-und-sprachen/veranstaltungen/afrikalinguistischeskolloquium/papers-sommersemester-2010/fipa-a-first-look-at-dialectal-variation-and-syntactic-variation}
\end{itemize}
\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Scripture Earth listing (the edition listed may be Swahili rather than Fipa; unverified): \url{https://www.scriptureearth.org/00eng.php?iso=fip}
\item Joshua Project profile (reports a Fipa New Testament): \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/11211/TZ}
\end{itemize}
\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Global Recordings Network Kifipa audio: \url{https://globalrecordings.net/en/language/fip}
\end{itemize}
\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The language is not known to be taught in schools. A curriculum would need to start from oral and scripture-derived material and new orthographic work.
\end{document}
